INT. VICTORIAN NURSERY - NIGHT

A tall, narrow room at the top of a stair. Gaslight burns low. A rocking horse, a dollhouse, a cradle with its hood drawn forward. The wallpaper is a pattern of faded roses. A window rattles in its frame.

MRS. PENELOPE ASHWORTH (40s, grey silk, a bunch of keys at her waist) stands in the doorway holding a candle. Behind her, her stern-faced maid, BRIDGET OLDACRE (20s, white cap, apron), clutches a coal scuttle.

PENELOPE
Bridget, did you wind the music box?

BRIDGET
No, ma'am.

PENELOPE
Then who did?

A tinkling melody drifts from the cradle. The hood is drawn forward. Neither woman moves.

BRIDGET
It's the draught, ma'am.

PENELOPE
There is no draught, Bridget.

The tune slows. Stumbles. A tiny creak as the cradle rocks once.

BRIDGET
(whispering)
Ma'am, the baby's been in the churchyard since March.

PENELOPE
I know it.

She steps forward. The candle gutters. The rocking stops.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
(softly)
Hush, my darling. Mother's here.

The hood of the cradle lifts, ever so slightly, from within.

CUT TO BLACK.
